#{[17592186230082 1029 "In this topic, we will practice drawing conclusions based on the margin of error for population proportions and evaluate what they suggest about the accuracy and reliability of survey results."] [17592186230083 1029 "Lines of best fit help us predict unknown values from data that follow a linear pattern.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice making these predictions."] [17592186230084 1029 "Questions about best-fit lines often focus on interpreting their slope. In this topic, we’ll practice estimating and interpreting slopes of best-fit lines to understand trends."] [17592186230085 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions about best-fit lines often contain graphs with axes that are not evenly scaled.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice reading and reasoning about such graphs."] [17592186230086 1029 "Problems with time-series graphs usually test the ability to interpret changes over time.\n\nHere, we’ll practice reading these trends and reasoning through patterns."] [17592186230087 1029 "Questions about the average rate of change often test our ability to measure how one quantity changes compared to another.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice estimating and calculating average rates of change from different types of plots."] [17592186230088 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice analyzing data using box plots by identifying and estimating means, comparing distributions, and calculating differences in ranges."] [17592186230089 1029 "This topic helps us practice in comparing datasets presented as dot plots. We will analyze their spreads, standard deviations, and relative variability."] [17592186230090 1029 "An important skill needed for the SAT math section is recognizing how symmetry influences the mean and median of a dataset. Here, we will find missing values and compare measures of centrality using data, both from raw data and from tables."] [17592186230091 1029 "This topic will help develop our skills in comparing measures of centrality using data presented in frequency tables."] [17592186230092 1029 "Questions about unit conversions often appear in real-world contexts and require paying attention to conversion factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice converting from smaller units of length to larger ones."] [17592186230093 1029 "Questions about unit conversions for mass and volume test how well we can move between different measurement systems. In this topic, we’ll practice converting between these units."] [17592186230094 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, problems about conversions between mixed units test whether we can convert rates by setting up corresponding factor-label chains.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice these conversions."] [17592186230095 1029 "In this lesson, we’ll practice multi-step unit conversions."] [17592186230096 1029 "Questions about mixed units with squares are common on the math section of the SAT exam. In this topic, we'll practice these types of conversions."] [17592186230097 1029 "Multistep rate problems often combine two or more relationships. These questions test our ability to reason through proportional relationships logically rather than relying on a single formula.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice breaking down complex rate problems into smaller steps, identifying what each rate represents, and using consistent units to solve them."] [17592186230098 1029 "Speed problems are among the most common applications of rate reasoning, often involving the relationship between distance, speed, and time.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving these problems systematically: translating words into equations, handling unit conversions, and mastering the logic behind distance–rate–time relationships."] [17592186230099 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, ratio problems often require applying a given ratio to find an unknown quantity.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving proportions to find missing values."] [17592186230100 1029 "In this topic, we’ll practice solving equations representing proportional relationships for a particular variable."] [17592186230101 1029 "When solving ODEs numerically:\n\n- The solution is advanced in many steps.\n- Each step introduces a small error (LTE).\n- These errors propagate forward (contributing to GTE).\n\nStability describes how a numerical method responds to these small perturbations.\n\nA method is **stable** if small errors remain controlled as the computation proceeds.\nA method is **unstable** if small errors can grow rapidly, even when the true solution stays well-behaved.\n\nStability determines how local errors turn into global errors: a method may be accurate in a single step, but if it is unstable, the global error can grow without bound.\n\nOne way to study stability is to test a numerical method on the following **test equation**:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThe exact solution of the test equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{-\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies $y(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n\nWe say that a numerical method with update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nis **stable** for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation, the numerical solution also satisfies\n\n$$\ny_n \\to 0 \\:\\text{as}\\: n \\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTo see how this works in practice, let's analyze the stability of Euler's method."] [17592186230102 1029 "We know how to apply the implicit Euler method to initial value problems\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthat are linear in $y$, that is, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x)\n$$\n\nHowever, in general, $f$ can be a nasty, nonlinear function in $y$. For example, consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - xy^4,\\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCan we apply the implicit Euler method with step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nto this IVP to approximate the value of $y(0.5)$?\n\nYes, we can! However, doing so requires defining a function $g$ to which the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a solution of the equation\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new})=0\n$$\n\nThen, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}$.\n\nTo see how, let's start by applying the implicit Euler method as we normally would. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= ((x_{new})^{2} - x_{new}(y_{new})^{4}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 0.5(x_{new})^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the update formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 0.5(x_{new})^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{4} \\\\\n0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{4} + y_{new} - y - 0.5(x_{new})^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with terms such as $0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4$ and $y_\\text{new}$, there’s no simple algebraic rearrangement, and solving exactly is impractical.\n\nInstead, let's define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} - y - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that, at any step, $y$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}$.\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find $y_\\text{new}$, we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new}=0+0.5=0.5\n$$\n\nSo, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot (y_{new})^{4} + y_{new} - 1 - 0.5 \\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.25(y_{new})^{4} + y_{new} - 1.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$"] [17592186230103 1029 "Problems involving fractions of fractions may also appear on the math section of the SAT exam to test the ability to simplify layered rational expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice reducing complex fractional expressions step by step, focusing on recognizing structure, factoring common terms, and rewriting them as simple rational forms."] [17592186230104 1029 "Problems involving simplifying exponential expressions often require applying the laws of exponents. In this topic, we’ll practice factoring, combining, and simplifying exponential terms using these laws."] [17592186230105 1029 "Questions involving exponential expressions in conjunction with the distributive law could appear on the math section of the SAT exam.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice applying the distributive property with expressions containing integer and fractional exponents."] [17592186230106 1029 "In this topic, we will find lower and upper bounds for the mean, median, and range from frequency tables or histograms, and determine the largest or smallest possible differences between related statistics."] [17592186230107 1029 "In this topic, we'll focus on finding missing observations based on the mean, median, or mode of a dataset."] [17592186230108 1029 "A typical question that often crops up on the SAT math section involves understanding how modifying data affects statistical measures.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice determining how adding, subtracting, or adjusting values changes statistics such as the mean, median, range, and standard deviation."] [17592186230109 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may be asked to demonstrate how changes to data affect the line of best fit.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice recognizing these effects so we can adjust the corresponding equations of the lines."] [17592186230110 1029 "Questions about residuals usually test the ability to compare an actual point to the corresponding point on the line of best fit.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying overestimates and underestimates, including identifying data points that struggle to fit the general trend."] [17592186230111 1029 "In this topic, we’ll practice spotting whether a situation describes steady, additive change (linear) or rapid, multiplicative growth or decay (exponential)."] [17592186230112 1029 "Questions about percentages represented by variables test our ability to form and solve equations involving unknown percentages.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving equations that connect parts, wholes, and percentages."] [17592186230113 1029 "In this lesson, we’ll practice solving multistep percentage problems that include successive markups and discounts, reversed percentage relationships, and finding original prices or costs."] [17592186230114 1029 "Questions about applying the successive percentage changes test our ability to handle multiple percent increases or decreases in sequence. These problems often appear in real-world scenarios.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice combining percentage changes."] [17592186230115 1029 "Ratio questions appear in many settings, such as rates with variables, geometry, and word problems.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice turning real-world and algebraic descriptions into proportions, then solving them."] [17592186230116 1029 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150$, and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40% \\cdot 150 &= \\frac{40}{100} \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 0.4 \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60$.\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25% \\cdot 60 &= \\frac{25}{100} \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 0.25 \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15$."] [17592186230117 1029 "In previous lessons, we learned how to apply percentage increases and decreases. We can refer to percentage increases and decreases collectively as **percentage change.** In this lesson, we'll learn how to find what percentage change is applied to a number to get another number.\n\nTo set the stage, let's first recall how to calculate a percentage increase in a retail situation.\n\nSuppose that a fast-food meal in a particular restaurant costs $\\[math]10$. The manager then decides to increase the price by $20\\%$. What is the new price of the meal?\n\nRecall that *increasing* the price of the meal by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 20\\% = 120\\%\n$$\n\nThus, the new price is $120\\%$ of the old price.\n\nExpressing $120\\%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n120\\% = \\dfrac{120}{100} = 1.2\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\[/math]10 \\times 1.2 = \\[math]12\n$$\n\nSo, the price of the meal after the increase is $\\[/math]12$."] [17592186230118 1029 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\[math]180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\[/math]150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\[math]180\\,000 - \\[/math]150\\,000 = \\[math]30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\[/math]180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\[math]150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage error &= \\frac{\\mid estimate - exact value\\begin{vmatrix}}{exact value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 180000 - 150000 \\mid}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 30000\\end{vmatrix}}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{30000}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{15} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high."] [17592186230119 1029 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\[/math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\[/math]300{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]300 \\cdot (100% + 35%) &= \\[/math]300 \\cdot 135% \\\\\n&= \\[math]300 \\cdot 1.35 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]405✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186230120 1029 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount.\n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change.\n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is\n\n$$\n1.5x\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books."] [17592186230121 1029 "In a previous lesson, we spent some time learning how to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This is known as finding a *percentage increase.*\n\nWe can apply similar reasoning to find a number that is a certain percentage *less than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a **percentage decrease.**\n\nTo apply a percentage decrease, we do the following:\n\n1. Subtract the decrease from $100\\%$.\n2. Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n3. Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nLet's take a look at a worked example."] [17592186230122 1029 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\[/math]228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\[/math]240{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]240 \\cdot (100% - 5%) &= \\[/math]240 \\cdot 95% \\\\\n&= \\[math]240 \\cdot 0.95 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]228✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186230123 1029 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\[math]10$ and the new price is $\\[/math]12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\[math]12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\[/math]10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid new value - original value\\begin{vmatrix}}{original value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 12 - 10 \\mid}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2\\end{vmatrix}}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier."] [17592186230124 1029 "We wish to construct the general solution to the first-order linear inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nThere are several ways to find the general solution to a first-order inhomogeneous ODE. The method we'll discuss here is a three-step process that involves finding the sum of the so-called complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe three steps are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**) $y_c(x)$ that satisfies the associated homogeneous ODE\n$a(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = 0$.\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. This is a single function that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n$y(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)$.\n\nAs we'll see, this method generalizes to higher-order linear ODEs, so it's well worth understanding how it works for first-order ODEs.\n\nLet's begin by practicing finding the complementary solution to an equation."] [17592186230125 1029 "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with **constant coefficients**\n\n$$\na y' + b y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants (\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n).\n\nAs we saw in a previous lesson, one method for finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x)$, we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it.\n\nAn ODE exhibits **sinusoidal forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is a sine or cosine function, or a combination of both. It is typically written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = C \\cos(\\omega x) + D \\sin(\\omega x)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ and $D$ are constants (not both zero).\n\nNow, if we choose only a sine or only a cosine as a trial function for the particular solution, differentiation will introduce the other sinusoidal term, since the derivative of a sinusoid is another sinusoid. Substituting such a trial solution into the ODE therefore produces both sine and cosine terms.\n\nTo ensure that this combination can match the forcing term $f(x)$, the trial guess *must* include both sine and cosine terms of the same frequency $\\omega$, even when $f(x)$ itself contains only one of them.\n\nTherefore, our trial guess for the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(\\omega x) + \\beta \\sin(\\omega x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with sinusoidal forcing. Let's look at a concrete example."] [17592186230126 1029 "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants and $a\\ne0$.\n\nAs we saw in a previous lesson, one method for finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x)$, we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it.\n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant.\n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example."] [17592186230127 1029 "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x)$. When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x$.* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx}$, then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx}\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example."] [17592186230128 1029 "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x)\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x)$.\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x)$.\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor $ay^{\\prime}+by=f(x)$ with constant coefficients $a\\ne0$ and $b\\ne0$, if $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n$, we try a polynomial particular solution of degree $n$. If $b=0$, direct integration instead gives a polynomial of degree $n+1$. Variable coefficients require a separate check of an appropriate trial form. After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial."] [17592186230129 1029 "Now that we've practiced finding both the complementary and particular solutions, let's put them together to find the **general solution**.\n\nRecall that the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ comes from solving the associated homogeneous equation. It contains an arbitrary constant, so it represents all homogeneous solutions.\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ is one specific function that satisfies the full inhomogeneous equation.\n\nTo find the general solution, we add these two pieces:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nThe particular solution already satisfies the full equation.\nAdding any complementary solution preserves this property while introducing the arbitrary constant needed to match different initial conditions.\n\nIn this way, the formula above describes *every possible solution* of a linear inhomogeneous equation.\n\nLet's consider an example."] [17592186230130 1029 "We will now check the stability of Euler's method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nEuler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n, y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nApplying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{n + 1} &= y_{n} + (- λy_{n}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= y_{n} - λy_{n} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (1 - λΔx)y_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Here, since $y_0=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{n} &= (1 - λΔx)y_{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (1 - λΔx)((1 - λΔx)y_{n - 2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - λΔx)^{2}y_{n - 2} \\\\\n&= ⋮⋮ \\\\\n&= (1 - λΔx)^{n}y_{0} \\\\\n&= (1 - λΔx)^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's define\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n$$\n\nThen, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$. The function $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is called the **stability function** of Euler's method.\n\nExpanding this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - λΔx \\mid \\mid < 1; - 1 < 1 - λΔx\\begin{vmatrix}< 1 \\\\ -2 < - λΔx\\end{vmatrix}< 0; 0 < λΔx \\mid < 2\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$.\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of Euler's method for the cases $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda\\Delta x = 2\n$$\n\nwith $\\lambda > 0$ fixed.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6685/Source/Images/f723b0574e30e665499be59c3e6e5136.png)\n\nNotice that, for $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$, the numerical values converge to $0$ (stable), whereas for\n\n$$\n\\lambda\\Delta x = 2\n$$\n\nthe values oscillate between $-1$ and $1$ and do not converge to $0$ (unstable).\n\nFor values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 2$, the numerical solution would grow in magnitude at each step, leading to rapidly diverging values."] [17592186230131 1029 "In general, when a numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nwe can write the update in the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the **stability function** of the method.\n\nFor stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Here, since $y_0=1$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{n} &= R(λΔx)y_{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (R(λΔx))^{2}y_{n - 2} \\\\\n&= ⋮⋮ \\\\\n&= (R(λΔx))^{n}y_{0} \\\\\n&= (R(λΔx))^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a geometric sequence, this decays to $0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ if and only if $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$.\n\nThe set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the **stability region** of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called **absolutely stable**.\n\nWe've seen that Euler's method is *not* absolutely stable, since its stability region is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$. But what about the implicit Euler method? Let's find out."] [17592186230132 1029 "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y\n$$\n\n(where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $\\mid R(\\lambda \\Delta x) \\mid < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |"] [17592186230133 1029 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}\n$$\n\n?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b] \\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$, then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t)$. Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x$, but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{1}e^{-tx}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}(e^{-tx})dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1} - xe^{-tx}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant."] [17592186230134 1029 "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{n + 1} &= y_{n} + (- λy_{n + 1}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\ny_{n + 1} + λy_{n + 1} \\cdot Δx &= y_{n} \\\\\ny_{n + 1}(1 + λΔx) &= y_{n} \\\\\ny_{n + 1} &= \\frac{1}{1 + λΔx} \\cdot y_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid \\frac{1}{1 + λΔx} \\mid \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0$. So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value\n\n$$\n\\lambda\\Delta x = 2\n$$\n\nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0$.\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6685/Source/Images/a3f116666d9e9be9489eec23582bcc71.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods."] [17592186230135 1029 "We know how to apply the trapezoidal method to initial value problems that are linear in $y$. Can we apply it to more complex initial value problems, such as\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2 - y^4, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nYes, we can. To do so, we define a function $g$ such that the next $y$-value, $y_\\text{new}$, satisfies the equation\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new})=0\n$$\n\nThen, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's apply the trapezoidal method to the IVP above with step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nWe begin as usual. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2x^{2} - y^{4} + 2(x_{new})^{2} - (y_{new})^{4}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 0.5x^{2} - 0.25y^{4} + 0.5(x_{new})^{2} - 0.25(y_{new})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the update formula for $\\Delta y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 0.5x^{2} - 0.25y^{4} + 0.5(x_{new})^{2} - 0.25(y_{new})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2 = 0\n$$\n\nHere, there is no simple algebraic rearrangement to isolate $y_\\text{new}$, so solving exactly is impractical.\nInstead, we define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that $x$, $y$, and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants at each step, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}$.\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find $y_\\text{new}$, we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 0 + 0.5 = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.25(y_{new})^{4} + y_{new} + 0.25 \\cdot (1)^{4} - 1 - 0.5 \\cdot (0)^{2} - 0.5 \\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.25(y_{new})^{4} + y_{new} - 0.875\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new})=0\n$$"] [17592186230136 1029 "Consider a body of mass $m$ falling vertically, influenced only by gravity $g$ and drag $D$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6693/Source/Images/1ddfcf47b0f79407927852ffef5ceb5a.png)\n\nAt higher speeds, or for larger objects, drag is often approximately proportional to the square of the body’s velocity. This model is called **quadratic drag**.\n\nAssume that both $m$ and $g$ are constant, and choose the downward direction as the positive direction.\n\nTwo forces act on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg$.\n- The drag force is\n$D=-kv^2$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality (this force is negative because it acts upward, opposite the motion).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is\n\n$$\nF = mg - kv^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= mg - kv^{2} \\\\\nma &= mg - kv^{2} \\\\\nm\\frac{dv}{dt} &= mg - kv^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\nm\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + kv^2 = mg \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{k}{m}v^2 = g}\n$$"] [17592186230137 1029 "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving homogeneous systems of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix.\n\nFor example, consider the system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = 5y(t); y^{′}(t) =-5x(t)\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that given the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nSince we have already seen how to compute the standard fundamental matrix, we will not repeat the calculation here. So, computing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (5t) & \\sin (5t) \\\\ -\\sin (5t) & \\cos (5t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= Φ_{0}(t)c \\\\\n&= e^{At}c \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (5t) & \\sin (5t) \\\\ -\\sin (5t) & \\cos (5t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (5t) \\\\ -\\sin (5t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin (5t) \\\\ \\cos (5t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n**Note:** Recall that for the general solution of the ordinary differential equation\n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t) \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x(t) = c \\, e^{at}, \\quad c \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nNow, for the general solution of the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}, \\quad \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples."] [17592186230138 1029 "A differential equation is a **d'Alembert differential equation** if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nNote that, in the special case where\n\n$$\nf(y') = y'\n$$\n\nthe equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The equation\n$y = x(y')^2 + \\dfrac{1}{y'}$\nis a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = (y')^2, \\qquad g(y') = \\dfrac{1}{y'}$.\n- The equation\n$y = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{y' - x}$\nis *not* a d'Alembert differential equation because the term $\\sin x$ prevents it from being written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$.\n\nLet's see more examples."] [17592186230139 1029 "Now, consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-2\\right) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2 - 3\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSuppose we apply the substitution $y'(x) = t$. Using the techniques shown earlier in the lesson (differentiating with respect to $x$), we can reduce this to the following first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac12 x = 2t-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nUsing the method of integrating factors, we compute $I(t)$ based on the coefficient\n\n$$\nP(t) = -\\dfrac12{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nI(t) = e^{\\int -1/2\\,\\textrm dt} = e^{-t/2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ converts the left-hand side into an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-t/2}\\frac{dx}{dt} - \\frac{1}{2}xe^{-t/2} &= (2t - \\frac{3}{2})e^{-t/2} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(xe^{-t/2}) &= (2t - \\frac{3}{2})e^{-t/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxe^{-t/2} &= ∫(2t - \\frac{3}{2})e^{-t/2}dt \\\\\nxe^{-t/2} &= 2∫te^{-t/2}dt - \\frac{3}{2}∫e^{-t/2}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts for the first integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxe^{-t/2} &= 2(-2t - 4)e^{-t/2} - \\frac{3}{2}(-2)e^{-t/2} + C \\\\\nxe^{-t/2} &= (-4t - 8)e^{-t/2} + 3e^{-t/2} + C \\\\\nxe^{-t/2} &= (-4t - 5)e^{-t/2} + C \\\\\nx(t) &= -4t - 5 + Ce^{t/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to construct the function $y$, we simply substitute $y' = t$ and $x = x(t)$ into our original equation. Note that the solution $y(t)$ is dependent on the parameter $t$.\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186230140 1029 "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving *inhomogeneous systems* of ODEs as well. Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, and suppose we are given an initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\n\nOur goal is to derive the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the *inhomogeneous term* by a change of variables. We define\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand differentiate it using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(t) &= (e^{-A(t - t_{0})})^{′}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}x^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (-A)e^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}(Ax(t) + g(t)) \\\\\n&=-Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t) \\\\\n&= e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate both sides from $t_0$ to $t$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nNext, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to the initial variable $\\mathbf{x}(t)$. To do that we multiply both sides by $e^{A(t-t_0)}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{A(t - t_{0})}y(t) &= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0}) + e^{A(t - t_{0})}∫_{t_{0}}^{t}e^{-A(s - t_{0})}g(s)ds \\\\\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0}) + ∫_{t_{0}}^{t}e^{A(t - t_{0})}e^{-A(s - t_{0})}g(s)ds \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0}) + ∫_{t_{0}}^{t}e^{A(t - t_{0}) - A(s - t_{0})}g(s)ds \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0}) + ∫_{t_{0}}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our IVP is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}}\n$$\n\nwhere the first term represents the solution of the corresponding *homogeneous system*, and the second term gives a particular solution of our *inhomogeneous system*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples."] [17592186230141 1029 "Before proceeding with the next example, let's recall some important properties of matrix exponentials:\n\n1. *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n$e^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}$.\n2. *Inverse:* For any $t$, the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and\n$(e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}$.\n3. *Identity:* For any square matrix $A$,\n$e^{A\\cdot 0} = I$.\n4. *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n$e^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}$.\n**Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute.\n\nLet's see how these properties can be used in practice."] [17592186230142 1029 "Now, let's find the solution to the initial value problem below\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = 7x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7y(t)\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nEvaluating both sides of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nat $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= Φ_{0}(0)c \\\\\nx(0) &= e^{A(0)}c \\\\\nx(0) &= Ic \\\\\nc &= x(0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0)\n$$\n\nComputing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{7t} & te^{7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}x(0) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{7t} & te^{7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(3 - 2t)e^{7t} \\\\ -2e^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186230143 1029 "Next, let's find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-7y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7x(t)\n$$\n\ngiven that $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7t) & -\\sin (7t) \\\\ \\sin (7t) & \\cos (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nWe can determine the constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$ using the initial condition at time $t=t_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c} \\implies \\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nsince the inverse of $e^{At_0}$ is $e^{-At_0}$. Plugging this back into our general solution gives the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0}) \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our specific problem (with $t_0=1$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - 1)}x(1) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7(t - 1)) & -\\sin (7(t - 1)) \\\\ \\sin (7(t - 1)) & \\cos (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4\\sin (7(t - 1)) \\\\ -4\\cos (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186230144 1029 "Recall that the *terminal velocity* is the constant speed a falling object approaches when the upward resistive force (such as drag) becomes large enough to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\n\nIf drag is proportional to the square of velocity, with a constant of proportionality $k > 0$, this balance occurs when\n\n$$\nmg - k(v_\\text{term})^2 = 0\n$$\n\nSolving for the terminal velocity, we get\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{v_\\text{term} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{mg}{k}}}\n$$\n\nLet's determine a terminal velocity in the next example."] [17592186230145 1029 "Suppose a body of mass $125\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second,\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nseconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality\n\n$$\nk=49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}\n$$\n\nand acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nAssuming the acceleration due to gravity is\n\n$$\ng=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nand the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag, let's find the equation for the velocity $v(t)$.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 125g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -49v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 125g - 49v^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n125g - 49v^{2} &= 125 \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - 0.392v^{2} \\\\\n&= g - 0.04gv^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g(1 - 0.04v^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - 0.04v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} &= g \\\\\n∫\\frac{1}{1 - 0.04v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}dt &= g∫dt \\\\\n∫(1)/(1 - (0.2v)^{2})dv &= g∫dt \\\\\n\\frac{1}{0.2}artanh(0.2v) &= gt + C_{1} \\\\\nartanh(0.2v) &= \\frac{1}{5}gt + C \\\\\n0.2v &= \\tanh (\\frac{1}{5}gt + C) \\\\\nv(t) &= 5\\tanh (\\frac{1}{5}gt + C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= 5\\tanh (\\frac{1}{5}g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n0 &= 5\\tanh (C) \\\\\n\\tanh (C) &= 0 \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 5\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{5} g t\\right)\n$$"]}
